findings.body:
The header size is now read with a bare `malloc_usable_size((void *)o)` call instead of `zmalloc_size()`, which is the wrapper the rest of the codebase uses. On the default Linux build (`MALLOC=jemalloc`, `src/Makefile:80-82`), `zmalloc.h` takes the jemalloc branch and never includes `<malloc.h>`. The jemalloc headers don't map `malloc_usable_size` to `je_malloc_usable_size` either, so the name is undeclared in `object.c`. The PR description says the fix is `zmalloc_size((void *)o)`, and that is what the code should call.

With GCC 14+ or Clang 16+ under `-std=gnu11`, an undeclared function is a hard compile error. With older GCC it is only a warning, and the call binds to glibc's `malloc_usable_size` with an implicit `int` return. That reads a chunk header before the pointer that jemalloc allocations don't have, so the reported size is garbage. macOS also has no `malloc_usable_size`, only `malloc_size`, so this breaks that build too.

The `zmalloc.h` comment at lines 146-148 warns that `malloc_usable_size()` is "for diagnostics only" and should not be used this way. I haven't compiled the tree, so the exact failure mode depends on the toolchain.

::diff{file="src/object.c" lines="1210-1218"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The PR description and every other size call in `src/` use the zmalloc wrapper. This line is the only one that doesn't.

::suggestion{file="src/object.c" lines="1217-1217"}
    size_t asize = zmalloc_size((void *)o);

The quicklist branch changed its divisor from `elecount` (total entries across the sampled nodes) to `samples` (number of sampled nodes). `ql->count` is the total number of entries in the whole list (`src/quicklist.h:110`), so the correct per-entry cost is `elesize / elecount`. Dividing by `samples` gives a per-node cost, and multiplying that by the entry total overstates the result by about the number of entries per node.

Worked example: a list of 10,000 small entries spread across about 20 listpack nodes of about 8 KB each. Five sampled nodes give `elesize` of about 40 KB and `elecount` of about 2,500. The original formula yields about 16 B/entry x 10,000, which is about 160 KB and matches reality. The new formula yields about 8 KB per node x 10,000 = about 80 MB, roughly 500 times too large. The PR description only asks for the header and key to be included, so this change is unexplained.

::diff{file="src/object.c" lines="1229-1240"}

::callout{type="warning"}
No existing test would catch this. The list test only asserts `> 0`, and the helper `memory_usage` returns a constant 1 off jemalloc (see the test finding).

::suggestion{file="src/object.c" lines="1239-1239"}
            asize += (double)elesize/elecount*ql->count;

In the stream branch, the average listpack size is multiplied by `s->rax->numele`, which the PR changed from `numele-1`. The code then adds the real size of the last node separately (`raxSeek(&ri,"$"...)` then `asize += zmalloc_size(ri.data)`). With `numele`, that last node is counted twice whenever the sampled-average branch runs.

The comment directly above the branch still says the average covers the "first N-1 listpacks" and the last one is added separately, so the code and comment now disagree. The impact is one listpack's average per large stream, so it is small, but the change is unexplained and goes against the comment.

::diff{file="src/object.c" lines="1311-1331"}

::suggestion{file="src/object.c" lines="1330-1330"}
            asize += lpsize * (s->rax->numele-1);
